{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Anarchy, Chaos.'' ''Anarchy'' is an absence of government; ''chaos'' is an absence of order.}}

'''anarcotin, anarcotine''' (a-när′kō-tin), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a-''<sup>18</sup>) + ''narcotic'' + ''-in''<sup>2</sup>, ''-ine''<sup>2</sup>.] A name proposed for narcotine, because of its apparent freedom from narcotic properties.

'''anareta''' (an-ar′e-tä̤), ''n.'' [ML., prop. *''anæreta'', < Gr. ἀναιρέτης, destroyer, murderer: see ''Anæretes.''] In ''astrol.'', the lord of the eighth house; the killing planet.
{{smaller block|The length of time which the apheta and ''anareta'', as posited in each respective figure of a nativity, will be in forming a conjunction, or coming together in the same point of the heavens, is the precise length of the native’s life.
{{right|''Sibley'', Astrology.}}}}

'''anaretic''' (an-a̤-ret′ik), ''a.'' [Prop. *''anæretic'', < Gr. ἀναιρετικός, destructive, with ref. to ''anareta'', q. v.] In ''astrol.'', destructive; killing: with reference to the anareta.
{{smaller block|The ''anaretic'' or killing places are the places of Saturn and Mars, which kill according to the direction of the hyleg to the succeeding signs.
{{right|''Sibley'', Astrology.}}}}

'''anaretical''' (an-a̤-ret′i-ka̤l), ''a.'' Same as ''anaretic.'' ''Sibley.''

'''Anarhynchus''' (an-a̤-ring′kus), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀνά, up, back, + ῥύγχος, snout, bill.] A remarkable genus of plovers, differing from all other birds in having the end of the bill bent sidewise and upward, but otherwise quite like ordinary plovers. ''A. frontalis'', the only species, is a native of New Zealand. Also spelled ''Anarrhynchus.'' ''Quoy and Gaimard'', 1833. See cut under ''plover''.
